"Because the cycle is so regular, people have used the Moon as a calendar for thousands of years. The traditional Chinese calendar begins each month on the day of the new moon, which is why the Mid-Autumn Festival, on the fifteenth day of the eighth month, always comes near a full moon. The phases also tell you when to look up. A full moon rises around sunset and stays up all night. A first-quarter moon is already up in the afternoon, and a last-quarter moon rises around midnight and is still in the sky the next morning. So the Moon is not only a nighttime object. This week, try looking for it in daylight.": "683a14ee98b7",
