"Many people, including many adults, believe the phases are caused by Earth's shadow falling on the Moon. It is an easy mistake to make, but it cannot be right. Earth's shadow always points directly away from the Sun, so the only time it can reach the Moon is at full moon, when the Moon is on the far side of Earth. When that happens, we see a lunar eclipse, which comes only a few times a year at most and is over within hours. Most months, the Moon slips just above or below the shadow, because its orbit is tilted by about five degrees. And a crescent moon hangs near the Sun in the sky, nowhere near Earth's shadow.": "9249934f3327",
