"Nearly a thousand years ago, the Song dynasty poet Su Shi wrote that people have their sorrows and joys, their partings and reunions, just as the Moon is sometimes hidden and sometimes bright, sometimes full and sometimes not. On Mid-Autumn night, families across Taiwan look up at a round, bright Moon. A week later, the same Moon looks like half a coin, and a week after that it seems to vanish from the sky. People have watched this cycle for as long as there have been people. The explanation is surprisingly simple, and it has nothing to do with the Moon itself changing shape. The Moon makes no light of its own. Moonlight is really sunlight reflected off the Moon's gray, rocky surface. At every moment, the Sun lights up exactly one half of the Moon — the half that faces the Sun — while the other half stays dark. This is true on the night of a full moon, and it is just as true on a night when we cannot see the Moon at all. The Moon is always half lit. What changes is how much of that lit half we can see from Earth. The Moon travels around Earth in an orbit, and as it moves, our view of its sunlit half keeps changing. When the Moon is between Earth and the Sun, its lit half faces away from us and we see almost nothing. This is the new moon. A few days later, a thin sliver of the lit half comes into view: a crescent. About a week after the new moon, we see exactly half of the disk lit. This phase is called the first quarter, because the Moon is a quarter of the way through its cycle. When Earth is between the Sun and the Moon, the whole lit half faces us, and we see a full moon. While the bright part is growing, we say the Moon is waxing; after the full moon it shrinks night by night, and we say it is waning. From one new moon to the next takes about 29.5 days. Many people, including many adults, believe the phases are caused by Earth's shadow falling on the Moon. It is an easy mistake to make, but it cannot be right. Earth's shadow always points directly away from the Sun, so the only time it can reach the Moon is at full moon, when the Moon is on the far side of Earth. When that happens, we see a lunar eclipse, which comes only a few times a year at most and is over within hours. Most months, the Moon slips just above or below the shadow, because its orbit is tilted by about five degrees. And a crescent moon hangs near the Sun in the sky, nowhere near Earth's shadow. Because the cycle is so regular, people have used the Moon as a calendar for thousands of years. The traditional Chinese calendar begins each month on the day of the new moon, which is why the Mid-Autumn Festival, on the fifteenth day of the eighth month, always comes near a full moon. The phases also tell you when to look up. A full moon rises around sunset and stays up all night. A first-quarter moon is already up in the afternoon, and a last-quarter moon rises around midnight and is still in the sky the next morning. So the Moon is not only a nighttime object. This week, try looking for it in daylight.": "f8df4014c8a4",
